                "text": "Let me understand the rule. Gravity pulls colored cells down vertically within each column. Let me verify with the example.\n\nInput:\nrow0: 0 5 0 9\nrow1: 6 0 0 0\nrow2: 0 5 0 9\nrow3: 0 0 0 0\n\nColumn 0: 0,6,0,0 -> non-zero: 6 -> falls to bottom: [0,0,0,6]\nColumn 1: 5,0,5,0 -> non-zero: 5,5 -> bottom: [0,0,5,5]\nColumn 2: 0,0,0,0 -> all zero\nColumn 3: 9,0,9,0 -> 9,9 -> bottom: [0,0,9,9]\n\nOutput rows:\nrow0: 0,0,0,0\nrow1: 0,0,0,0\nrow2: 0,5,0,9\nrow3: 6,5,0,9\n\nYes matches. So the verifier does this via rot270, sort by color... Actually it sorts each row so that x0 (mostcolor, 0) goes... let me not worry; the rule is gravity down per column.\n\nWait, careful: the verifier rotates, then partitions each row: sfilter keep identity==x0 (zeros) first, then sfilter flip (non-zeros) after. combine(a,b) = zeros followed by nonzeros. Hmm, that would put zeros first in each rotated row. Let's check: after rot270, columns become rows. Column 0 (0,6,0,0) becomes... rot270. Actually let's just trust the gravity interpretation which matched.\n\nNow I need to produce demonstration pairs. I'll create a variety of inputs showing gravity. Let me create several grids with scattered colored squares, various sizes.\n\nLet me make sure inputs are valid (verifier can evaluate). Non-empty grid, mostcolor presumably 0 (black field). I should keep 0 as the most common color. Let me create a few.\n\nPair 1: given example already? I should show my own. Let me create varied ones.\n\nExample A (4x4):\n[1,0,0,2]\n[0,3,0,0]\n[0,0,0,2]\n[0,3,0,0]\nCols: c0:1,0,0,0 -> [0,0,0,1]; c1:0,3,0,3 -> [0,0,3,3]; c2: all 0; c3:2,0,2,0 -> [0,0,2,2]\nOutput:\n[0,0,0,0]\n[0,0,0,0]\n[0,3,0,2]\n[1,3,0,2]\n\nLet me make a 5x5 with some columns having multiple different colors.\n\nExample B (5x5):\n[4,0,7,0,0]\n[0,0,0,8,0]\n[4,0,0,0,0]\n[0,6,7,0,9]\n[0,0,0,8,0]\n\nColumns:\nc0: 4,0,4,0,0 -> non-zero 4,4 -> bottom: [0,0,0,4,4]\nc1: 0,0,0,6,0 -> [0,0,0,0,6]\nc2: 7,0,0,7,0 -> [0,0,0,7,7]\nc3: 0,8,0,0,8 -> [0,0,0,8,8]\nc4: 0,0,0,9,0 -> [0,0,0,0,9]\n\nOutput rows:\nrow0: 0,0,0,0,0\nrow1: 0,0,0,0,0\nrow2: 0,0,0,0,0\nrow3: 4,0,7,8,0\nrow4: 4,6,7,8,9\n\nGood.\n\nExample C: a case where a column has stacked colors already? Let's include a horizontal bar.\n\nExample C (3x4):\n[2,2,2,0]\n[0,0,0,0]\n[0,0,0,3]\nColumns: c0:2,0,0->[0,0,2]; c1:2,0,0->[0,0,2]; c2:2,0,0->[0,0,2]; c3:0,0,3->[0,0,3]\nOutput:\n[0,0,0,0]\n[0,0,0,0]\n[2,2,2,3]\n\nExample D: 4x5 with mixed.\n[0,1,0,0,2]\n[3,0,0,4,0]\n[0,1,0,0,2]\n[0,0,5,0,0]\nCols:\nc0:0,3,0,0->[0,0,0,3]\nc1:1,0,1,0->[0,0,1,1]\nc2:0,0,0,5->[0,0,0,5]\nc3:0,4,0,0->[0,0,0,4]\nc4:2,0,2,0->[0,0,2,2]\nOutput:\nrow0: 0,0,0,0,0\nrow1: 0,0,0,0,0\nrow2: 0,1,0,0,2\nrow3: 3,1,5,4,2\n\nGood. I'll show maybe 4 examples. Let's do it.\n\nActually, the total number of pairs matters but the goal is inference. 3-4 diverse pairs good.\n\nLet me submit these via show_transformed_input. I'll do them in parallel.",
